e60fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192140 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{8} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{9}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192140 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192140 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b283ff6-f2a1-55a1-b214-edf220316fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192568 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192568 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cd/cd41e0c4d98c28e41c8e91743c70bbcac3e0b88071d1075e1cd518d2410843fa.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Write down this multiplication as a repeated addition of fraction models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56936482-95f9-54ff-b006-cd79247947ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192568 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{5}\n$$\n\nmultiplied by ${\\color{blue}3}$. This means that we need to make $\\color{blue}3$ copies of the given fraction model.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab193d9bb045165bcfffadae012523f33297548eacbdfd6b3514c791d7fac58.png)\n\nSo, the missing picture is:\n\n![](images/59/59f5625dce754f49724d16b00e839ec34e473601df4702db51f3973f14fd4c10.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192569 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192569 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/1553e601de2b1664996bb27d49cb09d3baacc72772f9846b45dc45dc9114ff53.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a8062c4-e4e5-5045-8b33-7f0afaf76d72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192569 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{6}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9443ceedbda3d541b0fc4d69489eead83dc8550710c7b6b3f1f9ed90d1dc97b.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 15\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192570 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192570 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a7/a7e53c31ac53ee142500771bf19af2995c02ecb6fef9aa37e73402e63fa7d518.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c170236-340b-5446-960f-a20b6937c23c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192570 #edb/ref 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b6a481c90b7372d0b78f7029b3fe8ae9c9c98f484459e1b7697308e3e2ebce.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $3$ shaded pieces, and the shape on the right has $6$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}2}$. (That is, the number of shaded pieces has been multiplied by ${\\color{blue}2}$.)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192858 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a891eaf9-6a4c-5e87-8aa0-95920347bd4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186192963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192859 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0abaf0f6-4018-54b2-a118-f420a3e0705c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192860 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192860 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192860 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number by Repeating the Shaded Parts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192860 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192860 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "78c0c75e-8a40-5064-a62a-954d8b85c3c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1045 "Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192881 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4d61d371-4b1d-53de-aa5b-4f42cd001c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193364 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a0/a013d4e6e0a7aced6662208bb2c1e42c46de68c0f4f8aef360585aa7ea805334.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c53f7f8f-8ef7-5317-a65a-a39660f0f70b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1ddc7cb84abc3e8a3fa7cea8e2d43d8cbee6e62e3ea02b99d024581d8b869282.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75df0560-ffa9-56b9-8620-1e3152afc6ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1061 88301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87fc1377-7af4-5e22-a70c-aed6997dce8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1061 88302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c0a507-52a5-5363-8641-411d16b13295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1061 88305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b181827-1135-5056-b586-5b3139e0ce58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1061 88308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d11338-8db9-53d5-b934-67da3916921a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1061 88309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ab5f02a-0507-5d82-88b7-8426ecc223d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}\n$$\n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\nMultiplying\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces.\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1065 8518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bfe48a90-960a-5004-8a63-a58ea72103ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4$.\n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n2 \\times 4 = 8\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1065 8524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbb3bf95-1b0b-52ea-b1ff-7a7a7c5d11f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Method for Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
